# Trivy triage list — see the header rules below.
#
# ISSUE #564 RESOLVED (2026-08): the time-boxed exceptions that lived
# here were the stale python:3.12-slim (trixie) digest findings from
# the #481 gate's first run (3 CRITICAL + 50 HIGH across 16 unique
# CVEs). The durable fix landed: the base moved to
# python:3.12-slim-bookworm @ sha256:a116514e…, which scans clean at
# the gate floor (CRITICAL,HIGH with --ignore-unfixed — the exact
# .github/workflows/ci.yml audit-job flags) with this file EMPTY, and
# all 16 entries were removed.
#
# Bookworm's remaining CRITICAL/HIGH CVEs (e.g. perl-base
# CVE-2026-13221, zlib1g CVE-2023-45853, libsqlite3-0 CVE-2025-7458)
# are all UNFIXED upstream — the gate's `ignore-unfixed: true` excludes
# them by design. They are enumerated for awareness in issue #564's
# decision comment, not triaged here; re-opening triage would just hide
# them from non-CI scans without making anyone safer.
#
# Rules: one CVE/GHSA per line; a justification comment + follow-up
# issue are required for any NEW entry; re-review on dependabot
# base-image bumps.
# rclone/rclone:1.75.0 (#686) — 8 HIGH in the image's Go stdlib/x/net,
# fixed in Go 1.25.13/1.26.6 but no rclone release ships a patched
# build yet. Triage rationale: short-lived CronJob-spawned archival
# pod, egress restricted to object storage TCP/443 + RFC1918/IMDS
# excepts (#477), non-internet-facing; DoS-class surfaces require
# crafted inbound inputs on protocols rclone does not serve. Drop
# these when a fixed rclone release ships (runbook: #642 step
# comments; watch issue #689).
